<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Donglin movement</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1604–1620s</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Donglin faction</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Donglin movement was a group of Confucian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scholar-officials">scholar-officials</a> of the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming dynasty</a> centred on the Donglin Academy at Wuxi, revived by Gu Xiancheng in 1604. Its members called for moral renewal and attacked corruption and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuch</a> power at court, and their factional struggles dominated politics under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wanli-emperor">Wanli Emperor</a> and his successors. Many were purged and killed by the eunuch Wei Zhongxian in the 1620s.</p></div>
